y be among the chosen evens. If zero is not chosen, then no special restriction beyond leading digit cannot be zero (but zero not present anyway). If zero is chosen, we must ensure zero is not placed in the leading position.\n\nThus we can separate cases:\n\nCase A: Zero is not among the chosen evens. So we choose 2 evens from {2,4,6,8} (size 4). Choose 3 odds from O (size 5). Then assign digits to positions with leading digit not zero (automatically satisfied because zero not present). So we need to count number of permutations of the selected 5 digits such that the first digit can be any of them (since none is zero). So total permutations = 5! for each selection.\n\nThus count_A = C(4,2) * C(5,3) * 5!\n\nCase B: Zero is among the chosen evens. So we choose zero plus one other even from {2,4,6,8} (size 4). So choose 1 from 4 for the other even. Choose 3 odds from O (size 5). Then assign digits to positions with leading digit not zero. So we need to count permutations of the selected 5 digits where zero cannot be in position 1.\n\nGiven a set of 5 distinct digits including zero, number of permutations where zero is not in first position = total permutations - permutations where zero is in first position. Total permutations = 5!. Permutations with zero in first position: fix zero at position 1, then arrange remaining 4 digits arbitrarily: 4! ways. So allowed permutations = 5! - 4! = 4! * (5 - 1) = 4! * 4 = 24 * 4 = 96. Indeed 5! = 120, 4! = 24, difference = 96.\n\nThus count_B = (choose zero + one other even) * C(5,3) * (5! - 4!). The number of ways to choose evens with zero: choose 1 from 4 = 4 ways. So count_B = 4 * C(5,3) * (5! - 4!).\n\nNow compute numbers:\n\nC(4,2) = 6. C(5,3) = 10. So count_A = 6 * 10 * 120 = 6 * 1200 = 7200? Wait 10*120 = 1200, times 6 = 7200. Yes.\n\nNow count_B: 4 * C(5,3) * (120 - 24) = 4 * 10 * 96 = 4 * 960 = 3840. Wait 10*96 = 960, times 4 = 3840.\n\nTotal = 7200 + 3840 = 11040.\n\nThus answer: 11040.\n\nBut we need to double-check if an